    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d734">

                <pb facs="0764" n="646" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg646"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/6218</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-02-13">February 13, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received February
                        23.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 914</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my telegram No. 8 of
                    January 4th (4 p.m.),<anchor n="62" xml:id="d734-f1"/> I have the honor to
                    transmit herewith a copy and an English translation <pb facs="0765" n="647"
                        xml:id="pg647"/> of a note from the Acting Minister of Foreign Affairs in
                    which he states that the President of Nicaragua, being desirous of laying the
                    foundation for lasting peace in the Republic by the practice of free
                    institutions and as the first step in a general plan aiming at the attainment of
                    truly free elections in the future, requests that the President of the United
                    States designate an American citizen to be appointed by the Supreme Court of
                    Nicaragua as President of the National Board of Elections.</p>
                <p>I have been informed by the President that, the Presidential elections of
                    November 1928 having been completed in accordance with the Executive Decree of
                    March 21, 1928, and General McCoy having resigned as President of the National
                    Board of Elections, the Dodds Electoral Law of 1923 has again come into
                    effect.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles C.
                    Eberhardt</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d734-a1.xml" xpointer="d734-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
